# Architecture & Code Quality

Choose boring, proven defaults unless the product clearly needs something special.

## Architecture

- Keep it simple: clear folders, separation of concerns, typed boundaries.
- Prefer small modules with explicit names over clever abstractions.
- Establish clean conventions early in greenfield repos and reuse them.
- When the repo already has patterns, match them — don’t fight the codebase.
- No drive-by refactors unrelated to the current task.

## Product states & quality

- Every user-facing flow handles **loading**, **empty**, and **error** states.
- Favor readability and maintainability over premature optimization.
- Keep types honest at module boundaries (API, DB, UI props).

## Accessibility basics

- Keyboard operable interactive elements
- Sufficient contrast
- Meaningful labels / `alt` text
- Visible focus states

## Security basics

- Never put secrets in client code or commit env credentials
- Validate/sanitize inputs at trust boundaries
- Use safe, standard auth patterns; don’t roll custom crypto

## DO / DON'T

- ✅ DO: `features/transactions/`, `lib/db.ts`, typed API responses
- ✅ DO: Show skeleton + empty CTA + recoverable error with retry
- ❌ DON'T: Giant `utils.ts` dumping ground or god-components
- ❌ DON'T: Silent `catch {}` or ignoring failed network calls
